  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/tylomelania-towutensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Tylomelania towutensis grows to about 8 cm in length. The shell is black, has 6-9 distinctly sculpted whorls, and the soft body is black with bright yellowish spots, which has led to the popular name gold-spotted snail.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/tylomelania-towutensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>The snails of the Malili Lakes on Sulawesi (formerly: Celebes) belong to the family Pachychilidae.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/tylomelania-towutensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>All Tylomelania species of the region have in common that they occur in warm (around 30°C), soft and alkaline water. However, in aquaristic practice they have proven to be very adaptable.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/tylomelania-towutensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>They are separately sexed animals, not hermaphrodites, but the sexes cannot be distinguished externally.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/tylomelania-towutensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>All Tylomelania species are viviparous and breathe through gills.</p>"
    }
  ],
